into cv/talk.amni
namespace talk
lang amni
use companion

Text side of the companion: splitting action tags out of a brain's reply, and an offline brain that answers with no network. No growable arrays: outputs are written into caller-provided byte slices (stop writing at the slice's end) and lengths are returned.

`tags_split(text const byte[:], clean byte[:], tags byte[:], lens i32[:]) int`: copy text into clean without its tags and write one line `kind:arg\n` per tag into tags (just `kind\n` when there is no arg); set lens[0] = clean length and lens[1] = tags length; return the tag count. A tag is `[[kind:arg]]` or `[[kind]]` where kind is 1 to 17 letters or '_' (lowercase it) and arg is up to 80 bytes without ']'. Also an action between single asterisks, `*...*` up to 40 bytes with no newline, is removed from clean and becomes a tag by the first match on its lowercased text: "wave" emote:wave, "danc" anim:dance, "nod" emote:nod, "laugh" or "giggl" emote:laugh, "bow" emote:bow, "jump" anim:jump, "spin" or "twirl" anim:spin, "sit" anim:sit, "wink" face:wink, "smil" or "grin" face:smile, "think" or "ponder" emote:think, "shrug" emote:shrug, "clap" emote:clap; otherwise it is just removed. Afterwards collapse runs of spaces in clean to one space and trim spaces at both ends.

A reply line is struct `Line { intent const byte[:], persona const byte[:], text const byte[:] }`. Persona "holo" is a bubbly, playful punk girl; "dex" is a calm engineer with dry wit; "*" lines are neutral and friendly and are used for any persona that has no line of its own for that intent. Placeholders: {name} the user's name, {cname} the companion's name, {time} like "2:05 PM", {day} the weekday, {tod} "morning" (hour < 12), "afternoon" (hour < 17) or "evening", {seen} the newest seen object, {arg} the event or command argument.

