`pick_line(intent const byte[:], persona const byte[:], seed int) const byte[:]`: among LINES with that intent and that persona, pick the (seed mod count)-th; when the persona has none, use the "*" lines; when the intent has none at all, return "".

Object categories by detector label: food for "banana", "apple", "sandwich", "orange", "broccoli", "carrot", "hot dog", "pizza", "donut", "cake", "cup", "bottle", "wine glass", "bowl"; pet for "dog", "cat", "bird", "horse", "teddy bear"; tech for "cell phone", "laptop", "keyboard", "mouse", "remote", "tv"; book for "book". `object_intent(label const byte[:]) const byte[:]` returns "ev_object_food", "ev_object_pet", "ev_object_tech", "ev_object_book" or "ev_object".

`intent_of(u const byte[:], arg byte[..70]&) const byte[:]` on the utterance u. When u starts with '@': the word after '@' up to a space is the event name and the rest (trimmed) goes to arg; "object" maps to `object_intent(arg)`, "open_palm" to "", any other name n to "ev_" + n when that intent exists in LINES, else "". Otherwise lowercase u, turn every non-letter, non-digit byte into a space, pad with one space on each side as w, and test in this order (a phrase matches when " phrase " occurs in w): name_set for "my name is X", "my name s X", "call me X" or "i m called X" where X is the next word (letters only, up to 24, first letter uppercased in arg; skip when there is no next word); name_ask for "what is my name", "what s my name", "do you know my name", "who am i"; greet for "hi", "hello", "hey", "yo", "hiya", "howdy", "good morning", "good afternoon", "good evening"; how_are_you for "how are you", "how r u", "how s it going", "how are things", "what s up", "whats up", "sup"; who_are_you for "who are you", "what is your name", "what s your name", "your name", "what are you"; time for "what time", "the time"; day for "what day", "the date", "today s date"; joke for "joke", "make me laugh", "something funny"; help for "what can you do", "help", "commands"; see for "what do you see", "can you see", "see me", "look at this", "what is this", "what s this"; cmd_dance "dance"; cmd_sit "sit", "sit down"; cmd_stand "stand up", "get up"; cmd_jump "jump"; cmd_wave "wave"; cmd_spin "spin", "turn around"; cmd_come "come here", "come closer", "come to me", "follow me"; cmd_home "go back", "go home", "back up"; cmd_roam "walk around", "explore", "wander"; cmd_stop "stop", "stay", "freeze"; cmd_look "look at me"; cmd_punch "punch", "fight"; cmd_bow "bow"; thanks "thank you", "thanks", "thx", "ty"; compliment "love you", "like you", "you re cute", "you re pretty", "you re cool", "you re awesome", "you re smart", "good job", "nice"; insult "stupid", "dumb", "hate you", "useless", "shut up"; bye "bye", "goodbye", "good night", "goodnight", "see you", "gotta go"; needs_brain "weather", "news", "search", "google", "look up"; ack when the whole trimmed text is one of "yes", "yeah", "yep", "sure", "ok", "okay", "no", "nope", "nah"; then fallback_q when the original u ends with '?' (ignoring trailing spaces), else fallback. A greeting followed by more words that match a later intent (like "hi what time is it") uses the later intent instead.

`brain_reply(input const byte[:], out byte[:]) int`: input starts with header lines `key=value` up to the first empty line, then the utterance. Keys: persona, name, cname, hour, minute, day, seen (comma separated, newest first), person ("1" when someone is on camera), seed. Find the intent with `intent_of`. Special cases: name_ask with an empty name uses name_unknown; see becomes see_obj when seen is not empty, else see_person when person is "1", else see_none. An empty intent returns 0. Pick the line with `pick_line(intent, persona, seed)`, then fill the placeholders: {name} = name, or "friend" when empty, except that a name_set line uses arg; {cname} = cname, or "your companion" when empty; {time} = hour and minute as 12-hour time like "2:05 PM" (hour 0 is 12 AM, 12 is 12 PM); {day} = day; {tod} from hour; {seen} = the first entry of seen; {arg} = arg. Write the result into out and return its length.
